{
  "dom_id": "step-q113656",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n14≡\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{A}}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\nI. $5$\n\nII. $6$\n\nIII. $24$",
  "worked_solution": "To visualize congruence modulo $10,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $10$ numbers. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14261972/assets/d73819e996f17cbfeeb548c44e63a40bd8987662a4f1d84fc40ac2f47285b5f9.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $10.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $10∤(14-5).$ Notice that $14-5=9,$ which is not divisible by $10.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $10∤(14-6).$ Notice that $14-6=8$ which is not divisible by $10.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $10∣(14-24).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(14-24)÷10=-10÷10=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$24$ only.\"",
